# 1D Mini-Batch Stochastic Gradient Descent

::: columns

::: {.column width=40%}

- Stochastic because each batch of data is drawn at random. 
- Nead a resonable value for the `learning_rate`.
  - too small, slow convergence
  - too large, diverges

::::

:::: {.column width=60%}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figures/ch02-18-gradient-descent}
\label{fig:ch02-18-gradient-descent}
\caption{SGD down a 1D loss curve (one learnable parameter) \parencite[pg.53]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}


::::

:::

# 2D Mini-Batch Stochastic Gradient Descent

::: columns

::: {.column width=60%}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figures/ch02-19-gradient-descent-2D}
\label{fig:ch02-19-gradient-descent-2D}
\caption{Gradient descent down a 2D loss surface (two learnable parameters). \parencite[pg.54]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::

:::: {.column width=40%}

- Gradient descent will be used in highly dimensional spaces.
- Every weight coefficient in a neural network is a free dimension in the space.
  - Tens of thousands or even millions of parameters.
- Cannot visualize, and intuitions from low-dimension examples can sometimes be misleading.

::::

:::

# SGD with Momemtum / Advanced Optimization Methods

::: columns

::: {.column width=50%}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.25]{figures/ch02-20-minimum-local-global}
\label{fig:ch02-20-minimum-local-global}
\caption{A local minimum and a global minimum. \parencite[pg.54]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::

:::: {.column width=50%}

- **Momentum** take into account previous weight updates rather than just looking at current gradient.
- SGD w/ momentum, Adagrad, RMSprop
- Momentum addresses two issues with SGD: convergence speed and local minima.
  - If enough momentum won't get stuck in local minimum
  - Will adjust learning rate to to make sure convergence does not take too long. 
- Implemented by moving each step based on both slope (current acceleration) and 
  velocity.
::::

:::
# Chaining Derivatives: The Backpropagation Algorithm

::: columns

::: {.column width=66%}

- **Backpropagation** is a way to use the derivatives of simple operations to easily
  compute the gradient of arbitrarily complex combinations of atomic operations.
- A NN consists of many tensor operations chained together, each of which has a simple
  known derivative.
- The chain rule states that \newline `grad(y, x) == grad(y, x1) * grad(x1, x)`
- Can be applied to any number of imtermediate functions

\vspace{0.1in}

```
grad(y, x) == (grad(y, x3)  * grad(x3, x2) *
               grad(x2, x1) * grad(x1, x))
```

::::

:::: {.column width=33%}

```python
def fg(x):
    x1 = g(x)
    y = f(x1)
    return y
```

\vspace{0.1in}

```python
def fghj(x):
    x1 = j(x)
    x2 = h(x1)
    x3 = g(x2)
    y = f(x3)
    return y
```

::::

:::

# Computation Graphs and Autometic Differentiation

::: columns

::: {.column width=33%}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.15]{figures/ch02-21-computation-graph-two-layer-model}
\label{fig:ch02-21-computation-graph-two-layer-model}
\caption{The computation graph representation of ch02 first look two-layer model. \parencite[pg.56]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::

:::: {.column width=66%}

- Useful way to think of and perform backpropagation: use computation graphs.
- A **computation graph** is a directed acyclic graph of operations.
- Treat computation as data:
  - Graph can be built in `TensorFlow` from a `GradientTape`
  - Represents forward pass
  - Can reverse links to perform backpropagation backward pass.
::::

:::

# Automatic Differentiation: Forward Pass

::: columns

::: {.column width=60%}

- Consider a simplified computation graph.
  - single layer all variables are scalar
  - scalar `w` for weights and `b` for bias
  - scalar `x` input
  - no activation function
  - loss is difference of result and `y_true`
- We want to update `w` and `b` in a way that will minimize `loss_val`.
  - want to compute `grad(loss_val, b)` and `grad(loss_val, w).
- Propagate these values to all nodes in the graph from top to bottom, 
  until we reach `loss_val`.
  - This is the **forward pass**


::::

:::: {.column width=40%}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.20]{figures/ch02-23-computation-graph-forward-pass}
\label{fig:ch02-23-computation-graph-forward-pass}
\caption{Running a forward pass. \parencite[pg.58]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::

:::

# Automatic Differentiation: Backward Pass / Backpropagation

::: columns

::: {.column width=50%}

- "reverse" the graph
  - For each edge from `A` to `B`, create opposite edge from `B` to `A`
  - backward graph represents the **backward pass**, a concrete implementation of
    performing **backpropagation**
  - You can obtain the derivative of a node with respect to another node by
    *multiplying the derivatives for each edge along the path linking the
    two nodes*.


::::

:::: {.column width=50%}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.20]{figures/ch02-24-computation-graph-backward-pass}
\label{fig:ch02-24-computation-graph-backward-pass}
\caption{Running a forward pass. \parencite[pg.58]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::

:::

# Automatic Differentiation using the Computation Graph

::: columns

::: {.column width=50%}

- Chain rule says the backword graph can be used to obtain the derivatives of the
  learnable parameters with respect to the loss.
- **Multiply the derivitives for each edge along the path linking two nodes**.
- For instance:
```
grad(loss_val, w) =
    grad(loss_val, x2) *
    grad(x2, x1) * 
    grad(x1, w)
```

::::

:::: {.column width=50%}

\begin{figure}
\includegraphics[scale=0.20]{figures/ch02-25-computation-graph-grad-loss-wrt-w}
\label{fig:ch02-25-computation-graph-grad-loss-wrt-w}
\caption{Path from ~loss\_value~ to ~w~ in the backward graph. \parencite[pg.59]{chollet-2021}}
\end{figure}

::::

:::

